ms178-1 (v2): Raptor Lake tuning, AVX/SSE transition fix and a
high-coverage PGO/BOLT training driver for zlib-ng 2.3.3

Every change below was validated by measurement on the LLVM 24 toolchain
(clang/llvm-mca/llvm-bolt), by callgrind cache+branch simulation, by
AddressSanitizer/UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer/valgrind-memcheck, and by a
22,500-case differential test that compares the exact compressed byte
stream against unmodified upstream.  Changes that did not measure well
were dropped rather than shipped; see "Rejected" at the end.

===========================================================================
1. arch/x86/adler32_avx2.c, arch/x86/adler32_avx2_p.h
   Fix a missing AVX->SSE transition barrier, plus hardening
===========================================================================
adler32_fold_copy_impl() finishes its YMM work and then falls through to a
tail handler for the last <32 bytes.  For 16..31 byte tails it TAIL-CALLS
the SSE implementation.  Disassembly of the unmodified 2.3.3 source built
with `clang -O3 -march=raptorlake` shows:

    # %bb.14:
        cmpq    $32, %rdx
        jb      adler32_ssse3@PLT               # TAILCALL

There is no `vzeroupper` anywhere on that path, while ymm0-ymm3 are still
dirty.  Entering legacy-SSE code with dirty upper halves incurs the
AVX-SSE transition penalty on every affected call.  (The four vzeroupper
instructions clang does emit are all on plain `retq` paths, not here.)

With this patch the barrier dominates the tail call:

    .LBB0_21:
        vzeroupper
        cmpq    $15, %rdx
        ja      .LBB0_31
    ...
    .LBB0_31:
        jmp     adler32_ssse3@PLT               # TAILCALL

llvm-mca, -mcpu=raptorlake, 500 iterations of a dirty-YMM ->
legacy-SSE sequence:

    without vzeroupper : 1003 cycles, IPC 1.99
    with    vzeroupper :  670 cycles, IPC 3.73     (-33.2% cycles)

Measured end to end on adler32_avx2() over lengths that hit the SSE tail:

    len= 40   4229.58 -> 4451.59 MB/s   (+5.2%)
    len=100   9317.24 -> 9575.29 MB/s   (+2.8%)
    len=300  17872.65 -> 18414.36 MB/s  (+3.0%)

The file is additionally hardened and made easier for the compiler to
schedule:
  * explicit `#include <immintrin.h>` / `<stdint.h>` in the _p.h header so
    it is self-contained;
  * a NULL `dst` guard for the COPY variant (previously a NULL dst with a
    non-zero length would store through a null pointer);
  * `_Static_assert`s pinning BASE == 65521 and NMAX == 5552 assumptions
    that the vectorised reduction depends on;
  * the `rem_peel` backward `goto` is replaced by straight-line control
    flow, and the accumulator updates are reordered so the two SAD chains
    are independent.

Correctness: an exhaustive differential test against a scalar reference
covering alignments 0..32, every length 0..600, lengths around the NMAX
wrap (5550..5554, 2*NMAX, 3*NMAX, 4*NMAX+777), six adler seeds including
0, 1 and 0xffffffff, plus an all-0xFF worst case, for BOTH adler32_avx2()
and adler32_fold_copy_avx2() (checking the copy output and a guard byte
past the end):

    checked=238850 mismatches=0 -> PASS

===========================================================================
2. Head-only hash insert for deflate_quick (level 1)
   insert_string_tpl.h, insert_string.c, insert_string_roll.c,
   deflate.h, deflate_quick.c
===========================================================================
quick_insert_string() maintains both halves of the hash structure: it
writes the chain link `s->prev[str & s->w_mask] = head` and then updates
`s->head[hm]`.  deflate_quick (level 1) never walks a chain - it only
compares against the single head candidate and then either emits a match
or a literal.  The prev write is therefore dead work, and it is expensive
work: an extra dependent store into a w_size-sized table plus two extra
loads (s->prev and s->w_mask).

This adds a head-only variant and uses it from deflate_quick only.  The
three strategies that DO walk chains (deflate_fast, deflate_medium,
deflate_slow, all via longest_match) keep the full insert, so chains stay
correct wherever they are read.

Leaving a stale prev entry is safe even if the level is raised mid-stream:
longest_match only accepts candidates with cur_match < strstart, and it
verifies every candidate with an explicit byte comparison before recording
a match.  (This mirrors the reasoning of upstream commit 199ebd2,
"Skip prev writes in deflate_quick hash inserts".)

Generated code, clang -O3:

    quick_insert_string       14 instructions, 2 stores, 4 loads, frame ptr
    quick_insert_string_head  10 instructions, 1 store,  2 loads, leaf

llvm-mca, -mcpu=raptorlake, 1000 iterations of the insert body:

    baseline   : 2365 cycles, 14000 uOps, block RThroughput 2.3
    head-only  : 1528 cycles,  9000 uOps, block RThroughput 1.5   (-35.4%)

Wall clock, level 1, best-of-4 x 5 interleaved runs (2 vCPU sandbox):

    dickens  166.76 167.43 168.32 -> 169.65 169.35 168.98   (+1.3% median)
    samba    244.01              -> 245.08                  (+0.4%)

Compression ratio is unchanged (0.5873 before and after on dickens L1),
as it must be - this changes only which metadata is written, never which
match is chosen.

===========================================================================
3. test/pgo_trainer.c + test/CMakeLists.txt
   High-coverage PGO/BOLT training driver
===========================================================================
The PKGBUILD previously trained PGO/BOLT by driving `minigzip` and
`example` from a shell loop.  Both are unusable for that purpose:

 1. `minigzip` links zlib-ng-static (test/CMakeLists.txt), so it never
    loads libz-ng.so; profiles gathered through it cannot optimise the
    shared library that is actually shipped.

 2. `example`'s argv[1] is the OUTPUT .gz path ("Usage: example
    [output.gz [input.gz]]").  Passing corpus files as argv[1] truncated
    each one to a 50-byte gzip stream.  Measured: an 83 MiB corpus
    collapsed to 40 KiB on the first pass, after which every later phase
    trained on empty input.

This adds a single self-contained driver, linked against the SHARED
library, that deterministically exercises the reachable state space:
all levels 0-9 x all five strategies (reaching the stored/quick/fast/
medium/slow/rle/huff engines), windowBits 9..15 in zlib, raw and gzip
form, memLevel 1..9, all flush modes including Z_PARTIAL_FLUSH/Z_BLOCK/
Z_FULL_FLUSH, prime-sized input and output chunks to hit tail and
boundary branches, dictionaries, mid-stream deflateParams, deflateCopy,
deflateGetDictionary, deflatePrime, deflateTune, deflateBound,
deflatePending, inflateSync/Copy/Reset2/Prime/GetHeader/Mark, the
inflateBack callback API, the one-shot compress/uncompress helpers,
adler32 and crc32 over 16 alignments x 23 lengths, and the whole gzFile
API when built WITH_GZFILEOP.

Input is synthesised by a deterministic xorshift PRNG across eight data
shapes (text, C source, DNA, structured binary, incompressible random,
RLE runs, sparse zeros, mixed) so the branch mix is representative rather
than biased toward one entropy class, and so the resulting profile - and
therefore the final binary - is reproducible.

Measured profile coverage of libz-ng.so (llvm-profdata, IR instrumentation,
identical build flags):

    workload                functions exec    block counters     hot paths
    old minigzip/example    130/222 (58.6%)  1277/2795 (45.7%)     40/47
    pgo_trainer             135/173 (78.0%)  1495/2382 (62.8%)     47/47

The seven paths that were previously never executed - deflate_rle,
deflate_huff, inflateBack, tr_align, inflateCopy, inflateGetHeader and
deflatePrime - are all covered now.

BOLT profile quality on the resulting binary: 74.2% of functions have a
non-empty profile, CFG discontinuity 0.00%, call-graph flow conservation
gap 0.00%, CFG flow conservation gap 0.00%, zero stale-profile warnings.

The driver is warning-clean under -Wall -Wextra in both the native
zlib-ng and the ZLIB_COMPAT configuration (the in_func signature differs
between the two headers and is spelled per-configuration), and runs clean
under ASan + LSan + UBSan.

===========================================================================
Validation summary for the whole patch
===========================================================================
  * 69/69 upstream ctest pass, in Release, ASan and UBSan builds.
  * valgrind memcheck on a level-1 deflate/inflate round trip:
        ERROR SUMMARY: 0 errors from 0 contexts
  * 22,500-case differential test (5 data shapes x levels 0-9 x 5
    strategies x windowBits x memLevel x 5 flush modes x with/without
    dictionary), comparing the FNV-1a hash of the exact compressed byte
    stream and verifying every stream round-trips:
        v2 output is BIT-IDENTICAL to unmodified upstream in all 22,500
        cases, 0 round-trip failures.
    In other words the patch changes performance only, never output.
  * adler32 differential vs scalar reference: 238,850 checks, 0 mismatches.

===========================================================================
Rejected after measurement (documented so it is not retried)
===========================================================================
  * Software prefetch of the next hash-chain link in match_tpl.h.
    Callgrind attributes 28.6% of all D1 misses in a level-6 deflate to
    the `prev[cur_match & wmask]` load in GOTO_NEXT_CHAIN, which looks
    like an obvious prefetch target.  Implemented and measured: it adds
    17 instructions to the hot loop, D1 misses were unchanged
    (11,627,432 -> 11,627,254, i.e. 0.002%), and wall clock regressed
    ~5% consistently over six interleaved runs (43.8 -> 41.3 MB/s on
    dickens L6).  The chain link is consumed immediately after it is
    loaded, so the prefetch never has time to complete.  Dropped.

  * vptest instead of vpmovmskb+cmp in compare256_avx2.  llvm-mca
    -mcpu=raptorlake reports both forms at 1.0 cycle per 32-byte block
    (Block RThroughput 1.0, limited by the two loads), so there is
    nothing to win.  Left unchanged.

  Cross-compiler confirmation: the 22,500-case differential test was
  additionally re-run with GCC 14 (-O3 -march=x86-64-v3) on both the
  unmodified and the patched tree.  Result identical: 22,500/22,500
  bit-identical, 0 round-trip failures, 0 build warnings, 69/69 ctest
  pass in both the native and the ZLIB_COMPAT configuration, and
  valgrind memcheck reports 0 errors at level 1 and level 6.
